## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test snippet demonstrating idempotency validation
async function processPayment(idempotencyKey, paymentPayload) {
  const lockAcquired = await redis.setnx(
    `payment:${idempotencyKey}`, 
    JSON.stringify({ status: 'processing', timestamp: Date.now() })
  );

  if (!lockAcquired) {
    return { status: 'duplicate', message: 'Request already processed' };
  }

  try {
    // Execute pipeline: gateway -> risk check -> ledger -> settlement
    const result = await executePaymentPipeline(paymentPayload);
    await redis.setex(`payment:${idempotencyKey}`, 3600, 
      JSON.stringify({ status: 'completed', result }));
    return result;
  } catch (error) {
    await redis.del(`payment:${idempotencyKey}`);
    throw error;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical webhook security testing skills at a beginner level, focusing on cryptographic verification and attack scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- HMAC verification
- Timing-safe comparisons
- Timestamp tolerance
- Replay protection
- Test harness usage

## Code Example

```javascript
// Node.js example
const crypto = require('crypto');
function sign(body, secret){
  return crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret).update(body).digest('hex');
}
function verify(body, ts, provided, secret){
  const expected = sign(body, secret);
  return crypto.timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(provided,'hex'), Buffer.from(expected,'hex')) && Math.abs(Date.now() - Number(ts)) < 300000;
}
```

